"Glossary - DCRM (RDA Edition) (https://bsc.rbms.info/DCRMR/glossary/)\nciteturn7view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view1\",\"lineno\":190}); Total lines: 438\nL173: ## B\nL174: \nL175: bibliographic description\nL176: A set of bibliographic data recording and identifying a resource, i.e., the description that begins with the title proper and ends with the last note. See also cite179†description .\nL177: \nL178: bibliographic format\nL179: The number of times the printed sheet has been folded to make the leaves of a book, e.g., folio (one fold giving two leaves), quarto (two folds giving four leaves), etc. See also cite180†gathering and cite181†signature .\nL180: \nL181: broadsheet\nL182: See cite182†single-sheet publication .\nL183: broadside\nL184: See cite182†single-sheet publication .\nL185: ## C\nL186: \nL187: cancellation\nL188: A substitution for something originally printed. Usually applies to leaves but may apply to portions of leaves, pairs of leaves, or entire gatherings. The replacement leaf, etc., is called the cancel or cancellans (plural cancels or cancellantia). The original leaf, etc., is variously called the cancelled leaf, the uncancelled leaf (if it is still present), the cancelland, or the cancellandum (plural cancellanda).\nL189: caption title\nL190: Title above a chapter, section, article, or the title beginning a textual resource without a title page. See also cite177†title .\nL191: \nL192: carrier\nL193: A physical medium in which text, data, sound, images, and other kinds of content are stored.\nL194: \nL195: catchword\nL196: A word under the last line of text on a page that repeats the first word on the following page.\nL197: \nL198: chief source of information\nL199: See cite183†preferred source of information .\nL200: chief title\nL201: The distinguishing word or sequence of words that names a resource, as given on the title page (or substitute). This definition excludes alternative titles, parallel titles, other title information, and subsidiary title information preceding the chief title on the title page, such exclusion resulting usually in a short title. See also cite177†title and cite184†title proper .\nL202: chronogram\nL203: A phrase, sentence, or other text in which the numeric values of certain letters (usually distinguished typographically or visually) express a date when added together.\nL204: \nL205: collection\nL206: A group of resources assembled by an agent from a variety of sources.\nL207: \nL208: collective title\nL209: A title of manifestation of an aggregate. A collective title is usually different from the titles of the expressions and works that are embodied by an aggregate. See also cite177†title .\nL210: colophon\nL211: A statement at the end of a resource giving information about one or more of the following: the title, author(s), publisher, printer, date of publication or printing. It may also include other information.\nL212: \nL213: conjugate leaves\nL214: Two leaves that form a single piece of paper, parchment, etc., in a gathering. See also cite185†leaf .\nL215: \nL216: container\nL217: A housing that is physically separable from the carrier or group of carriers being housed. See also cite186†carrier .\nL218: copy\nL219: In bibliographic description, a particular instance of a resource produced in multiples. See also cite187†item .\nL220: \nL221: corporate body\nL222: A collective agent that is composed of persons who are organized for a common purpose or activity.\nL223: \nL224: cover\nL225: A source of information in a resource that is a protective binding of a volume.\nL226: \nL227: cover title\nL228: A title printed on the cover of a resource as issued. See also cite177†title .\nL229: ## D\nL230: \nL231: description\nL232: A set of data that records and identifies one or more RDA entities. See also cite188†bibliographic description .\nL233: \nL234: device\nL235: A design—generally symbolic, emblematic, or pictorial rather than textual—used to identify an agent (e.g., artist, printer, bookseller, publisher). To be distinguished from a logo that renders a name as a stylized, primarily textual design.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGlossary - DCRM (RDA Edition) (https://bsc.rbms.info/DCRMR/glossary/)\nciteturn7view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view1\",\"lineno\":240}); Total lines: 438\nL218: copy\nL219: In bibliographic description, a particular instance of a resource produced in multiples. See also cite187†item .\nL220: \nL221: corporate body\nL222: A collective agent that is composed of persons who are organized for a common purpose or activity.\nL223: \nL224: cover\nL225: A source of information in a resource that is a protective binding of a volume.\nL226: \nL227: cover title\nL228: A title printed on the cover of a resource as issued. See also cite177†title .\nL229: ## D\nL230: \nL231: description\nL232: A set of data that records and identifies one or more RDA entities. See also cite188†bibliographic description .\nL233: \nL234: device\nL235: A design—generally symbolic, emblematic, or pictorial rather than textual—used to identify an agent (e.g., artist, printer, bookseller, publisher). To be distinguished from a logo that renders a name as a stylized, primarily textual design.\nL236: docket title\nL237: A title written, typed, or printed on a document, or on a label affixed to the document, briefly indicating its contents or subject. Usually found perpendicular to the main text on the verso of the last leaf on a document designed to be folded for filing.\nL238: \nL239: double leaf\nL240: A leaf of double size relative to the volume, sewn or tipped in with the fold at one of the outer edges of the volume, with blank pages facing each other, functioning as a single leaf. See also cite185†leaf .\nL241: ## E\nL242: \nL243: edition\nL244: All copies resulting from a single job of typographical composition or produced from essentially the same master copy.\nL245: \nL246: editor\nL247: An agent contributing to an expression of a work by revising or clarifying the content, by selecting and putting together works, or parts of works, by one or more creators; adding introduction, notes, or other critical matter; or preparing an expression of a work for production, publication, or distribution.\nL248: element\nL249: A specific aspect, characteristic, attribute, or relationship used to describe an RDA entity. See also cite189†area .\nL250: \nL251: errata\nL252: A list of errors in a printed work discovered after printing and shown with corrections.\nL253: \nL254: expression\nL255: An intellectual or artistic realization of a work in the form of alpha-numeric, musical, or choreographic notation, sound, image, object, movement, etc., or any combination of such forms.\nL256: extent\nL257: The first element of the physical description area. It gives the number and carrier units of the resource being described and, in some cases, other indications of the extent.\nL258: ## F\nL259: \nL260: facsimile\nL261: An illustrative content that consists of an exact copy of an original, usually in the same dimensions as the original, especially of books, documents, prints, and drawings. Today the original is often reproduced photographically or digitally; in the past, it was reproduced by engraving or another printmaking process.\nL262: fascicle\nL263: One of the temporary divisions of a printed resource that, for convenience in printing or publication, is issued in small installments, usually incomplete in themselves; they do not necessarily coincide with any formal division of the work into parts, etc. Usually the fascicle is protected by temporary paper wrappers. It may or may not be numbered. A fascicle is distinguished from a part by being a temporary division of a work rather than a formal component unit.\nL264: fingerprint\nL265: An identifier for an early printed resource that consists of a sequence of characters from internal sources of information specified by an established scheme.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGlossary - DCRM (RDA Edition) (https://bsc.rbms.info/DCRMR/glossary/)\nciteturn7view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view1\",\"lineno\":280}); Total lines: 438\nL262: fascicle\nL263: One of the temporary divisions of a printed resource that, for convenience in printing or publication, is issued in small installments, usually incomplete in themselves; they do not necessarily coincide with any formal division of the work into parts, etc. Usually the fascicle is protected by temporary paper wrappers. It may or may not be numbered. A fascicle is distinguished from a part by being a temporary division of a work rather than a formal component unit.\nL264: fingerprint\nL265: An identifier for an early printed resource that consists of a sequence of characters from internal sources of information specified by an established scheme.\nL266: ## G\nL267: \nL268: gathering\nL269: One or more pairs of leaves that form a distinctive unit for binding purposes. A gathering may consist of a folded sheet, a fraction of a folded sheet, or several folded sheets tucked inside one another. See also cite190†bibliographic format and cite181†signature .\nL270: \nL271: ## H\nL272: \nL273: half title\nL274: A title of a resource appearing on a leaf preceding the title page. See also cite177†title .\nL275: ## I\nL276: \nL277: illustration\nL278: Visual elements relating to the content of the resource. For decorative elements that do not illustrate the content of the resource, see cite191†ornaments .\nL279: \nL280: impression\nL281: All copies of a textual manifestation produced in the course of one printing event; the term is synonymous with “printing.”\nL282: \nL283: inscription\nL284: Text hand-lettered on an item prior to acquisition by the repository, especially when for documentation or commemoration.\nL285: integral\nL286: 1. A leaf is integral to a gathering if it is conjugate with another leaf in the gathering. 2. A leaf is integral to a publication if it can be assumed to be present in all copies represented by the bibliographic description. Leaves added after publication by an owner (e.g., extra-illustrated copies) or by a binder are not considered integral to the publication and thus must be described in item-specific notes.\nL287: issue\nL288: A group of published copies of a textual manifestation which constitutes a consciously planned publishing unit, distinguishable from other groups of published copies by one or more differences designed expressly to identify the group as a discrete unit.\nL289: \nL290: item\nL291: A single exemplar or instance of a resource.\nL292: ## J\nL293: \nL294: ## K\nL295: ## L\nL296: \nL297: leaf\nL298: A unit of extent that forms a subunit of a volume; each leaf consists of two pages, one on each side, either or both of which may be blank. See also cite192†conjugate leaves and cite193†double leaf .\nL299: \nL300: leaf of plates\nL301: A plate in a resource that also has one or more leaves or pages of text (whether preliminary text or text proper). Plates may be described in terms of pages of plates if they are numbered as pages or are unnumbered and have illustrative matter on both sides. See also cite194†plate .\nL302: letterpress\nL303: Printing done directly from raised surfaces. Includes printing done from type, from wood blocks (e.g., woodcuts and wood-engravings), and from metal surfaces designed for relief printing (e.g., stereotypes and electrotypes).\nL304: ## M\nL305: \nL306: manifestation\nL307: The physical embodiment of an expression of a work.\nL308: \nL309: manuscript\nL310: Writings (including musical scores, maps, etc.) made by hand, typescripts, and inscriptions on clay tablets, stone, etc.\nL311: \nL312: map\nL313: An illustrative content that consists of a representation, normally to scale and on a two-dimensional medium, of a selection of material or abstract features on, or in relation to, the surface of the earth, another celestial body, or an imaginary place.\nL314: monograph\nL315: A bibliographic resource that is complete in one part or intended to be completed within a finite number of parts. See also cite195†multipart monograph .\nL316: \nL317: monographic series\nL318: See cite196†series .\nL319: \nL320: multipart monograph\nL321: A bibliographic resource complete, or intended to be completed, in a finite number of separate parts. The separate parts may or may not be numbered. Examples of multipart monographs include multipart texts, map sets, and map series. See also cite197†monograph .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGlossary - DCRM (RDA Edition) (https://bsc.rbms.info/DCRMR/glossary/)\nciteturn7view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view1\",\"lineno\":370}); Total lines: 438\nL356: ## R\nL357: \nL358: RDA entity\nL359: An abstract class of key conceptual objects in the universe of human discourse that are a focus of interest to users of RDA metadata in a system for resource discovery. An RDA entity includes an agent, collective agent, corporate body, expression, family, item, manifestation, nomen, person, place, timespan, and work.\nL360: recto\nL361: 1. The page of a resource intended to be read after its facing page (i.e., the right-hand page for left-to-right reading scripts and the left-hand page for right-to-left reading scripts), usually bearing an odd page number if the volume is paginated. 2. The side of a printed sheet intended to be read first. See also cite200†verso .\nL362: \nL363: running title\nL364: A title, or abbreviated title, that is repeated at the head or foot of each page or leaf. See also cite177†title .\nL365: ## S\nL366: \nL367: series\nL368: A group of separate resources related to one another by the fact that each resource bears, in addition to its own title proper, a collective title applying to the group as a whole. The individual resource may or may not be numbered. See also cite201†subseries .\nL369: series title page\nL370: An added title page bearing a series title proper and usually, though not necessarily, other information about a series such as statement of responsibility, numeric designation, data relating to publication, title of the resource within the series. See also cite202†added title page and cite176†title page .\nL371: signature\nL372: A letter, numeral, symbol, or a group of such characters, printed at the foot of the rectos of the first few leaves of an intended gathering for the purpose of aiding binders in correctly assembling the sections. See also cite190†bibliographic format and cite180†gathering .\nL373: single-sheet publication\nL374: A publication printed on a single or composite piece of paper or other material; it may be printed on one or both sides and may be bound or unbound. The content of a single-sheet publication, as here defined, is predominantly textual in nature, though it may contain illustrations that are subordinate or coordinate to the text.\nL375: \nL376: source of information\nL377: A source (e.g., title page, colophon, container) of a data value, or content, for an element.\nL378: standard number\nL379: The International Standard Book Number (ISBN) or any other internationally agreed upon standard number that identifies a resource uniquely.\nL380: \nL381: state\nL382: A copy or a group of copies of a printed textual sheet or a publisher’s casing which differs from other copies (within the same impression or issue) of that sheet or casing in any respect which the publisher does not wish to call to the attention of the public as representing a discrete publishing effort.\nL383: statement of responsibility\nL384: A statement relating to the identification or function of agents responsible for the creation of, or contributing to the realization of, the intellectual or artistic content of a resource.\nL385: \nL386: subseries\nL387: A series within a series, that is, a series that always appears in conjunction with another, usually more comprehensive, series of which it forms a section. Its title may or may not be dependent on the title of the main series. See also cite196†series .\nL388: ## T\nL389: \nL390: tactile text\nL391: A content type consisting of content expressed through a form of notation for language intended to be perceived through touch. Braille text and other tactile forms of language notation are included.\nL392: title\nL393: A word, phrase, character, or group of characters, normally appearing in a resource, that names the resource or the work contained in it. See also cite203†alternative title , cite204†caption title , cite205†chief title , cite206†collective title , cite207†cover title , cite208†half title , cite209†other title information , cite210†parallel title , cite211†running title , cite184†title proper , and cite212†variant title .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPublishing your book with the University of Chicago Press (https://press.uchicago.edu/resource/publishing_with_chicago.html)\nciteturn7view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6view2\",\"lineno\":105}); Total lines: 196\nL97: To guide you through this stage, we provide detailed instructions on\nL98: \nL99:   * cite12†manuscript preparation ,\nL100:   * cite13†art requirements , and\nL101:   * cite14†securing permissions .\nL102: You will work closely with your editorial associate or assistant editor on final submission of these materials. At this stage, you should also inform us of any specific requests you have for copyediting or design, and if there are aspects of the manuscript that will require special attention. We will also want to know your suggestions for the cover and any critical scheduling details, such as an upcoming review for tenure or promotion.\nL103: Once your manuscript, art, and permissions are finalized, your editorial associate or assistant editor will pass everything along to your production team. We call this moment “transmittal.” Typically, two weeks after transmittal, all the Press staffers involved with your book (about a dozen people) will meet to launch the book, reviewing the schedule, marketing, design, metadata, and other details. This is also when your book’s title and pricing are finalized.\nL104: Your editor will follow up after that with many details for you about the process to come.\nL105: ## Production: Meet Your Production Editor\nL106: \nL107: At this point, your acquisitions team will take a step back. Your production editor will be your point of contact as your book is copyedited, designed, typeset, and proofread.\nL108: #### cite36†Copyediting L109: The production editor will oversee the copyediting (or, in some cases, they might serve as the copy editor themselves). Your copy editor will go through your manuscript carefully, correcting grammar, style, and typos; they will ask you a variety of questions, but they do not typically do the kind of conceptual work you may have done with your acquisitions editor.\nL110: You will have two or three weeks to review the copyeditor’s work and to further refine your manuscript, but this is your last opportunity to make substantive changes.\nL111: #### cite36†Design and Typesetting L112: Meanwhile, your designer is working on the cover and interior design. We have our own staff of talented, professional designers, who work closely with marketing and acquisitions staff, and we pride ourselves on our distinctive book designs. Your editor welcomes your suggestions for the design before transmittal and will share them for consideration with the designer and the rest of the Press. We will send you the design before making it official.\nL113: Once the cover is finalized and appears on your book’s page on our website, please feel free to circulate it widely!\nL114: #### cite36†Proofs and Indexing L115: \nL116: The last stage of production before your book is printed and sent out into the world is proofreading and indexing. We will send you page proofs, which you will proofread for typos and other minor errors.\nL117: This is also when you will prepare the index. If you are new to indexing, never fear: your production editor will send you detailed instructions. If you prefer to hire a freelancer to prepare the index for you, your production editor can provide you with names of some of our regulars if you let us know in advance.\nL118: ## Beyond Production: Meet Your Promotions Manager\nL119: \nL120: Back when your book was transmitted, your acquisitions editor introduced you to the book’s promotions manager.  We are unusual in having promotions managers dedicated to specific subject areas and experienced in positioning books within and across related fields.\nL121: #### cite36†Marketing and Publicity L122: \nL123: Your promotions manager will send you a detailed questionnaire that you should fill out carefully and completely, covering subjects like awards, conferences, review copies, and more.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCirculation Policy | Halifax Public Libraries (https://www.halifaxpubliclibraries.ca/about/library-policies/circulation-policy/)\nciteturn7view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6view4\",\"lineno\":357}); Total lines: 546\nL347: To access the cardholder’s account, those who have been provided with authorized access will be asked to present acceptable ID or their own library card to match their name to that listed in the account. They are not required to have their own library account unless they are borrowing holds on behalf of the original requestor. If those with authorized access contact the library by telephone, their own library card is required to properly match them to the account.\nL348: Once the Authorized Access person presents acceptable ID or their own library card, they can do the following on a card owner’s account:\nL349:   * Access the borrowing information\nL350:   * Renew items\nL351:   * Place requests\nL352:   * Pay fees and obtain receipts\nL353:   * Report a card lost/stolen\nL354:   * Make changes to the borrower account information, with the exception of child accounts, where only the signing adult can assign/alter Authorized Access names on the account\nL355:   * Pick up holds for the card owner, with items checked out on the card belonging to the person with authorized access.  Exceptions: Interlibrary Loans must be checked out on the requester’s account.\nL356: Provided that the card has not been reported lost or stolen, access to the account will be granted to anyone with the card in hand, whether or not they are listed as having authorized access.\nL357: ## Use of Library Card\nL358: \nL359: Signing the library card, a library card registration form, or by selecting “I agree” on the online library card application form implies that the card holder assumes full responsibility for the use of the card.  All cardholders are responsible for:\nL360:   * Materials checked out on their card\nL361:   * Promptly reporting changes in account information\nL362:   * Promptly reporting the loss or theft of a library card\nL363:   * Adding or removing those with authorized access to a library account\nL364:   * The use of the library card to access public computers and technology\nL365: \nL366: An adult who signs a child’s card, library card registration form, or selects “I agree” on the online library card application, assumes full responsibility for the use of a child’s account.\nL367: In lieu of a library card, identification can be shown and provided a match can be made to a library account, material may be borrowed.\nL368: \nL369: Customers with a full borrowing card have a limit of 60 physical items that may be checked out on an account at any one time.  Customers issued a limited borrowing card may borrow up to 5 items. Home Delivery clients are exempt from the 60 item limit.\nL370: These limits do not include items borrowed from the Library’s digital collections. Digital content providers may have borrowing limits specific to those collections.\nL371: \nL372: Children 0-13 years of age are not permitted to place holds on or borrow films classified as 14A, 18A, or R (Restricted). Youth 14-17 years of age are not permitted to place holds on or borrow films classified as 18A or R (Restricted).\nL373: \nL374: The Library is not responsible for any damage caused to audio-visual equipment by library materials.\nL375: Customers are responsible for monitoring their library card activity, including items checked out and due dates. Customers can access account information online on the Library’s website or by contacting library staff. Email and telephone notifications are provided as a courtesy. Customers with a working email address or telephone number listed in their account will receive either telephone or email notifications to alert them of the following:\nL376:   * Overdue notices\nL377:   * Holds notification\nL378:   * Lost items\nL379:   * When account charges exceed $100\nL380:   * Pre-notification of due dates (email notification only)\nL381: ## Holds and Returns\nL382: \nL383: Items not available at a particular branch, out on loan or not yet available (new items on order), can be placed on hold and sent to a preferred Halifax Public Libraries location, provided that item allows requests. Customers will be placed on a waiting list and those with an email address or telephone number listed in their accounts be notified when the item becomes available. Requested items are held for a total of 7 days. Customers have the option to pause (defer) holds.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCirculation Policy | Halifax Public Libraries (https://www.halifaxpubliclibraries.ca/about/library-policies/circulation-policy/)\nciteturn7view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6view4\",\"lineno\":381}); Total lines: 546\nL370: These limits do not include items borrowed from the Library’s digital collections. Digital content providers may have borrowing limits specific to those collections.\nL371: \nL372: Children 0-13 years of age are not permitted to place holds on or borrow films classified as 14A, 18A, or R (Restricted). Youth 14-17 years of age are not permitted to place holds on or borrow films classified as 18A or R (Restricted).\nL373: \nL374: The Library is not responsible for any damage caused to audio-visual equipment by library materials.\nL375: Customers are responsible for monitoring their library card activity, including items checked out and due dates. Customers can access account information online on the Library’s website or by contacting library staff. Email and telephone notifications are provided as a courtesy. Customers with a working email address or telephone number listed in their account will receive either telephone or email notifications to alert them of the following:\nL376:   * Overdue notices\nL377:   * Holds notification\nL378:   * Lost items\nL379:   * When account charges exceed $100\nL380:   * Pre-notification of due dates (email notification only)\nL381: ## Holds and Returns\nL382: \nL383: Items not available at a particular branch, out on loan or not yet available (new items on order), can be placed on hold and sent to a preferred Halifax Public Libraries location, provided that item allows requests. Customers will be placed on a waiting list and those with an email address or telephone number listed in their accounts be notified when the item becomes available. Requested items are held for a total of 7 days. Customers have the option to pause (defer) holds.\nL384: Most materials can be borrowed from and returned to any Halifax Public Libraries locations. Exceptions will be noted at the point of checkout.\nL385: \nL386: The Library may also have various community-based return locations. Because these locations are not emptied daily, items may show as being checked out on library card after they have been returned.\nL387: Halifax Public Libraries participates in the Libraries, Archives and Museums Nova Scotia (LAMNS) Borrower Anywhere / Return Anywhere (BARA) initiative, which allows library material to be returned to partnering public, community college or university libraries in Nova Scotia. Book Club Kits and Interlibrary Loan material borrowed from Halifax Public Libraries must be returned to a Halifax Public Libraries location. Other exceptions will be noted at time of checkout.\nL388: ## Loan Periods and Renewals\nL389: \nL390: It is the responsibility of the customer to ensure that materials are returned on time in order to make them available to others.\nL391: \nL392: Renewals\nL393: \nL394: Loan periods may be extended by renewing items. A renewal extends the due date by the same length of time as the original loan period.  The renewal period starts on the day the item is renewed, not the original date the item was due. There is a maximum of three renewals on each item.\nL395: \nL396: Items may not be renewed if:\nL397:   * Other borrowers have requested them\nL398:   * They have been renewed the maximum number of times\nL399:   * They are part of the Library’s non-renewable collections\nL400: \nL401: Summary of loan periods and renewals\nL402: \nL403: Item Type  | Loan Period  | Max Renewals\nL404:   * Books\nL405:   * Magazines\nL406:   * Kits\nL407:   * CDs & Audiobooks*\nL408:   * DVDs/Blu-Rays (over 300 minutes)\nL409:   * Uncatalogued Paperbacks\nL410:   * Other Special Collections (eg. Pedometers, Energy Saving Meters/Power Cost Monitors, Nordic Walking Poles, Thermal Leak Detectors, Musical Instruments, etc.)\nL411:  | 21 days  | 3\nL412: DVDs/Blu-rays (300 minutes and less)  | 7 days  | 3\nL413:   * Child and Youth Browsing Collection Books\nL414:   * High Demand and Browsing DVDs/Blu-Rays (300 minutes and less)\nL415:  | 21 days  | 0\nL416: 7 Day Loan Books, Rapid Reads, Browsing DVDs/Blu-Rays (300 minutes and less)  | 7 days  | 0\nL417: Book Club Kits  | 42 days  | 0\nL418: Light Therapy Lamps  | 14 days  | 3\nL419: Radon Detectors  | 42 days  | 3\nL420: Recreation Facility Passes  | 14 days  | 0\nL421: \nL422: *CD loan period may be reduced to 7 days based on demand\nL423: \nL424: Vacation and Special Loan Periods\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCirculation Policy | Halifax Public Libraries (https://www.halifaxpubliclibraries.ca/about/library-policies/circulation-policy/)\nciteturn7view7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6view4\",\"lineno\":388}); Total lines: 546\nL384: Most materials can be borrowed from and returned to any Halifax Public Libraries locations. Exceptions will be noted at the point of checkout.\nL385: \nL386: The Library may also have various community-based return locations. Because these locations are not emptied daily, items may show as being checked out on library card after they have been returned.\nL387: Halifax Public Libraries participates in the Libraries, Archives and Museums Nova Scotia (LAMNS) Borrower Anywhere / Return Anywhere (BARA) initiative, which allows library material to be returned to partnering public, community college or university libraries in Nova Scotia. Book Club Kits and Interlibrary Loan material borrowed from Halifax Public Libraries must be returned to a Halifax Public Libraries location. Other exceptions will be noted at time of checkout.\nL388: ## Loan Periods and Renewals\nL389: \nL390: It is the responsibility of the customer to ensure that materials are returned on time in order to make them available to others.\nL391: \nL392: Renewals\nL393: \nL394: Loan periods may be extended by renewing items. A renewal extends the due date by the same length of time as the original loan period.  The renewal period starts on the day the item is renewed, not the original date the item was due. There is a maximum of three renewals on each item.\nL395: \nL396: Items may not be renewed if:\nL397:   * Other borrowers have requested them\nL398:   * They have been renewed the maximum number of times\nL399:   * They are part of the Library’s non-renewable collections\nL400: \nL401: Summary of loan periods and renewals\nL402: \nL403: Item Type  | Loan Period  | Max Renewals\nL404:   * Books\nL405:   * Magazines\nL406:   * Kits\nL407:   * CDs & Audiobooks*\nL408:   * DVDs/Blu-Rays (over 300 minutes)\nL409:   * Uncatalogued Paperbacks\nL410:   * Other Special Collections (eg. Pedometers, Energy Saving Meters/Power Cost Monitors, Nordic Walking Poles, Thermal Leak Detectors, Musical Instruments, etc.)\nL411:  | 21 days  | 3\nL412: DVDs/Blu-rays (300 minutes and less)  | 7 days  | 3\nL413:   * Child and Youth Browsing Collection Books\nL414:   * High Demand and Browsing DVDs/Blu-Rays (300 minutes and less)\nL415:  | 21 days  | 0\nL416: 7 Day Loan Books, Rapid Reads, Browsing DVDs/Blu-Rays (300 minutes and less)  | 7 days  | 0\nL417: Book Club Kits  | 42 days  | 0\nL418: Light Therapy Lamps  | 14 days  | 3\nL419: Radon Detectors  | 42 days  | 3\nL420: Recreation Facility Passes  | 14 days  | 0\nL421: \nL422: *CD loan period may be reduced to 7 days based on demand\nL423: \nL424: Vacation and Special Loan Periods\nL425: Vacation Loans:  Borrowers are entitled to two six-week vacation loans within a 12 month period.  Only renewable materials available for regular 3 week loan may be checked out for a six week vacation loan period.  Interlibrary loan materials do not qualify for vacation loans.\nL426: Special Loans:  A longer than normal borrowing period is available to those whose employment requires them to be away beyond a normal loan period. Customers requiring a special loan period must provide identification as to the employer, for example, Armed Forces card, DFO identification; company ID, International Seamen’s Union, union card, etc.  Special loans may also be granted in cases of illness, extended medical treatments, etc. and will be considered on a case-by-case basis.\nL427: Only materials available for regular 3 week loan qualify for a special loan period.  Interlibrary loan materials do not qualify for special loans.\nL428: ## Charges for Lost and Damaged Items / Replacement Cards\nL429: \nL430: Lost\nL431: \nL432: When a borrower reports an item as lost, the replacement cost will be charged to their account. Any item 30 days or more overdue will automatically be considered lost and the replacement cost will be added to the borrower’s account. If a lost item is returned, the replacement cost is removed.\nL433: \nL434: Damaged\n"
